task: queue-swap
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/queue-swap
worktree: ../okay-wt-swap

why: the operator asked for one queue that is right for multi-multi.
  Six axes measured today are in tension by construction, so no single
  algorithm is ideal — but one TYPE can become the right one, the way
  the scheduler picks its policy by measuring. A channel starts as a
  plain ring (fastest at one producer, which is what a channel usually
  has) and installs a partitioned buffer whose PART 0 IS THAT RING the
  moment producers actually contend. No element moves.

what, staged:
  1. `SentinelChannel` holds its buffer in a volatile var, read once
     per operation into a local; an audit that nothing reads it twice
     and compares.
  2. the swap, on the SLOW path only: when a sender is about to park
     and another sender is already waiting, install the partitioned
     buffer (part 0 = the current ring), publish the widened waiter
     arrays BEFORE the buffer, then wake every parked sender — a
     sender waiting for room in part 0 must be able to see the new
     parts. One CAS: losers use the winner's buffer.
  3. opt-in first: `Queues.strong[A].growing`, with the laws. Only
     when the laws and the numbers hold does making it
     `Channel.apply`'s default become a separate proposal.

why the trigger is there and not on every send: a volatile read per
  send was measured today at 1.49x (the `owner0` experiment in
  adaptive-one-producer). The parking path already costs a park.

predict: one producer unchanged (the swap never fires; the channel IS
  a ring), four and sixteen producers within 15% of `adaptive` today
  (135 / 99), because after the swap it is the same buffer.
  Disqualifying: any law in TestManyToMany or TestChannelLaws failing
  under the swap, or a sender left parked after it — that is the
  hazard this design has, and if it cannot be made to hold, the entry
  says so and the ring stays.
